3a" 13194139535641 true] [17592186358971 1145 "To find the horizontal asymptote of a rational function, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by the dominant term in the denominator, and then we let $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nIgnoring the coefficient, the dominant term in the denominator is ${x}^{7}.$ So, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by ${x}^{7},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{7x+3}{6{x}^{7}-2{x}^{2}-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{7x}{{x}^{7}}+\\frac{3}{{x}^{7}})}{(\\frac{6{x}^{7}}{{x}^{7}}-\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{{x}^{7}}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{7}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{7}{{x}^{6}}+\\frac{3}{{x}^{7}})}{(6-\\frac{2}{{x}^{5}}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{7}})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when $x$ is very large, the terms $\\frac{7}{{x}^{6}},\\frac{3}{{x}^{7}},\\frac{2}{{x}^{5}}$ and $\\frac{1}{{x}^{7}}$ become very small. Therefore, as $x\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{(\\frac{7}{{x}^{6}}+\\frac{3}{{x}^{7}})}{(6-\\frac{2}{{x}^{5}}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{7}})} \\\\  & \\to \\frac{0+0}{6-0-0} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the horizontal asymptote is $y=0.$" 13194139535641 true] [17592186358972 1000 #edb/ref 17592186358973 13194139535641 true] [17592186358972 1000 #edb/ref 17592186358974 13194139535641 true] [17592186358972 1000 #edb/ref 17592186358975 13194139535641 true] [17592186358972 1000 #edb/ref 17592186358976 13194139535641 true] [17592186358972 1000 #edb/ref 17592186358977 13194139535641 true] [17592186358972 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535641 true] [17592186358972 1041 "selection" 13194139535641 true] [17592186358972 1104 #edb/ref 17592186358977 13194139535641 true] [17592186358972 1110 #uuid "d10d0f32-219d-5f98-a5f3-4582f493a66b" 13194139535641 true] [17592186358973 1034 "y=-1" 13194139535641 true] [17592186358973 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535641 true] [17592186358973 1111 #uuid "ea820507-e4a6-550e-80b5-4ae6cde10644" 13194139535641 true] [17592186358974 1034 "y=\\frac{7}{6}" 13194139535641 true] [17592186358974 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535641 true] [17592186358974 1111 #uuid "c8bb9674-11b4-5ee4-852b-d4ad672aa4a8" 13194139535641 true] [17592186358975 1034 "y=-\\frac{3}{2}" 13194139535641 true] [17592186358975 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535641 true] [17592186358975 1111 #uuid "aa9faeb4-78a2-5681-9eb0-bf6ae3d7e636" 13194139535641 true] [17592186358976 1034 "y=-3" 13194139535641 true] [17592186358976 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535641 true] [17592186358976 1111 #uuid "f1edc508-eac2-5d96-b5f5-647e52f9f349" 13194139535641 true] [17592186358977 1034 "y=0" 13194139535641 true] [17592186358977 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535641 true] [17592186358977 1111 #uuid "525be846-766c-5b21-9429-2c30da1a92ee" 13194139535641 true] [17592186358978 1017 #edb/ref 17592186358979 13194139535641 true] [17592186358978 1089 "q-113176" 13194139535641 true] [17592186358978 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535641 true] [17592186358978 1100 "Determine the horizontal asymptote of $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{8}-4{x}^{4}+2x}{{x}^{6}-1}.$" 13194139535641 true] [17592186358978 1113 #uuid "6076d1df-0a51-5be4-8eb1-e4f9aaadd8b8" 13194139535641 true] [17592186358978 1145 "To find the horizontal asymptote of a rational function, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by the dominant term in the denominator, and then we let $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nIgnoring the coefficient, the dominant term in the denominator is ${x}^{6}.$ So, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by ${x}^{6},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{8}-4{x}^{4}+2x}{{x}^{6}-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{{x}^{8}}{{x}^{6}}-\\frac{4{x}^{4}}{{x}^{6}}+\\frac{2x}{{x}^{6}})}{(\\frac{{x}^{6}}{{x}^{6}}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{6}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{({x}^{2}-\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{2}{{x}^{5}})}{(1-\\frac{1}{{x}^{6}})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when $x$ is very large, the terms $\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}},$ $\\frac{2}{{x}^{5}}$ and $\\frac{1}{{x}^{6}}$ become very small. Therefore, as $x\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{({x}^{2}-\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{2}{{x}^{5}})}{(1-\\frac{1}{{x}^{6}})} \\\\  & ≈\\frac{{x}^{2}-0+0}{1-0} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is not a constant, which means that $y=f(x)$ does not have a horizontal asymptote." 13194139535641 true] [17592186358979 1000 #edb/ref 17592186358980 13194139535641 true] [17592186358979 1000 #edb/ref 17592186358981 13194139535641 true] [17592186358979 1000 #edb/ref 17592186358982 13194139535641 true] [17592186358979 1000 #edb/ref 17592186358983 13194139535641 true] [17592186358979 1000 #edb/ref 17592186358984 13194139535641 true] [17592186358979 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535641 true] [17592186358979 1041 "selection" 13194139535641 true] [17592186358979 1104 #edb/ref 17592186358981 13194139535641 true] [17592186358979 1110 #uuid "de09a269-0bd3-5614-a91c-ea5aa354b65b" 13194139535641 true] [17592186358980 1034 "y=-1" 13194139535641 true] [17592186358980 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535641 true] [17592186358980 1111 #uuid "17bbe0a2-f33b-55d4-abe8-f5d3880e79a4" 13194139535641 true] [17592186358981 1034 "No horizontal asymptotes" 13194139535641 true] [17592186358981 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535641 true] [17592186358981 1111 #uuid "3fb24a27-f25c-5c92-a2b4-85eba6f7ee8b" 13194139535641 true] [17592186358982 1034 "y=0" 13194139535641 true] [17592186358982 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535641 true] [17592186358982 1111 #uuid "4dab9c13-38a6-55de-981e-ff634b08035d" 13194139535641 true] [17592186358983 1034 "y=-2" 13194139535641 true] [17592186358983 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535641 true] [17592186358983 1111 #uuid "aa2429b2-2299-5938-8580-2980ac815d47" 13194139535641 true] [17592186358984 1034 "y=1" 13194139535641 true] [17592186358984 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535641 true] [17592186358984 1111 #uuid "484feb8e-ebc4-5f36-a0a8-cb3ab1b50c99" 13194139535641 true] [17592186358985 1017 #edb/ref 17592186358986 13194139535641 true] [17592186358985 1089 "q-113183" 13194139535641 true] [17592186358985 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535641 true] [17592186358985 1100 "Determine the horizontal asymptote of $f(x)=\\frac{8{x}^{6}+3{x}^{2}-7}{{x}^{4}-6x}.$" 13194139535641 true] [17592186358985 1113 #uuid "46cdbc89-38e8-5e50-824f-ae7d2846257a" 13194139535641 true] [17592186358985 1145 "To find the horizontal asymptote of a rational function, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by the dominant term in the denominator, and then we let $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nIgnoring the coefficient, the dominant term in the denominator is ${x}^{4}.$ So, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by ${x}^{4},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{8{x}^{6}+3{x}^{2}-7}{{x}^{4}-6x} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{8{x}^{6}}{{x}^{4}}+\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{{x}^{4}}-\\frac{7}{{x}^{4}})}{(\\frac{{x}^{4}}{{x}^{4}}-\\frac{6x}{{x}^{4}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(8{x}^{2}+\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{7}{{x}^{4}})}{(1-\\frac{6}{{x}^{3}})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when $x$ is very large, the terms $\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}},$ $\\frac{7}{{x}^{4}}$ and $\\frac{6}{{x}^{3}}$ become very small. Therefore, as $x\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{(8{x}^{2}+\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{7}{{x}^{4}})}{(1-\\frac{6}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & ≈\\frac{8{x}^{2}+0-0}{1-0} \\\\  & =8{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is not a constant, which means that $y=f(x)$ does not have a horizontal asymptote." 13194139535641 true] [17592186358986 1000 #edb/ref 17592186358987 13194139535641 true] [17592186358986 1000 #edb/ref 17592186358988 13194139535641 true] [17592186358986 1000 #edb/ref 17592186358989 13194139535641 true] [17592186358986 1000 #edb/ref 17592186358990 13194139535641 true] [17592186358986 1000 #edb/ref 17592186358991 13194139535641 true] [17592186358986 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535641 true] [17592186358986 1041 "selection" 13194139535641 true] [17592186358986 1104 #edb/ref 1759